          body: "**Twelve** is not an accident of recruitment: a nation founded on twelve tribes hears in twelve apostles a claim that Israel is being refounded — Jesus says so outright later (19:28, *twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes*). The list runs in pairs (Mark says they were sent two by two), and it repays a slow read. Only this gospel attaches the epithet **ὁ τελώνης** — *the tax collector* — to Matthew's name, in the book that bears his name: whoever wrote it chose to preserve the founder-list with the shameful job title still on it, like a plaque that keeps the disgrace in the inscription. Four fishermen; a tax collector who took money for Rome; and **Simon the Cananaean** — from Aramaic *qan'ana*, the zealot, the party (or at least the temperament) of armed resistance to Rome. A collaborator and an insurgent in the same travelling party is not a detail a movement invents for plausibility; it is either remembered or unthinkable. And the list ends the only way this list could ever end, with the name the church could never stop writing: *Judas Iscariot, who also betrayed him* — kept on the roll, epitaph and all, in every gospel's version. This community remembered its origins without airbrushing.",
