      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a10.8.1',
          kind: "concept",
          refs: ['g10.8.9', 'g10.10.15'],
          lemma: "δωρεὰν ἐλάβετε, δωρεὰν δότε",
          title: "Freely — the economics of the mission",
          body: "Wandering wonder-workers were a recognized first-century profession, and they charged. Against that market, verse 8 sets the mission's entire business model in four Greek words: **δωρεὰν ἐλάβετε, δωρεὰν δότε** — *as a gift you received, as a gift give*. What follows is the packing list read as theology: no gold, no silver, not even the small copper; no bag (the πήρα may be the beggar's collecting-bag — i.e., *don't even take the equipment for receiving donations*); one tunic; no staff. The point is not asceticism for its own sake but legibility: a healer who cannot be paid cannot be suspected, and a messenger with no provisions is a walking demonstration that the kingdom he announces feeds its workers. Hence the balancing principle — *the worker deserves his food* — support, yes; fees, never. The line between those two is where religious movements have been shipwrecking ever since (the *Didache*, within a couple of generations, already has to rule that a visiting prophet who stays three days or asks for money is a fraud). The first missionary instruction in this gospel is, in effect: **the moment the gospel becomes a revenue stream, it has stopped being the gospel.**",
        },
      ],
