      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a10.29.1',
          kind: "concept",
          refs: ['g10.29.15', 'g10.30.9'],
          lemma: "ἄνευ τοῦ Πατρὸς ὑμῶν",
          title: "The sparrow still falls",
          body: "The ἀσσάριον was the smallest coin worth naming — a sixteenth of a denarius; sparrows were the cheapest flesh in the market, food for those who could afford nothing else (Luke's version adds that at bulk rates the fifth sparrow was thrown in free — worth precisely nothing). The argument is from the bottom up: if the throwaway bird registers, you register. But read the sentence carefully, because it is more austere than the comfort it is usually quoted for. It does **not** say the sparrow will not fall. It says the sparrow will not fall **ἄνευ τοῦ Πατρὸς ὑμῶν** — *without your Father* — and translators have struggled with the bareness of that ever since (many quietly supply ”without your Father's *will*” or ”*knowledge*”; the Greek supplies nothing). Sparrows fall. The promise, three verses before *do not fear those who kill the body*, cannot be that they don't — the whole paragraph assumes the disciples may die. What is promised is that no fall, however small and cheap, happens *outside the Father's presence* — unaccompanied, unwitnessed, unregistered down to the numbered hairs. Whether that is enough is the question this whole project keeps circling. The text, characteristically, does not oversell: it offers not an exemption but an accompaniment, and asks you to decide if that changes anything. Verse 31 thinks it does: *do not fear*.",
        },
      ],
