          title: "The paradox, and the cup of cold water",
          body: "The chapter's whole terrifying arc — wolves, floggings, betraying households, crosses — resolves into two closing movements worth reading together. First the paradox of verse 39, built on the word **ψυχή**, which is both ”life” and ”self”: *the one who finds his self will lose it*. This is not a puzzle for mystics; it is close to an observable law. The self curated as a possession — protected, optimized, insured against every loss — goes dead in the keeping; anyone who has met a person organized entirely around self-preservation has seen the corpse walking. And the self *spent* — handed over to something worth more than it — comes back alive. The gospel's outrageous addition is the phrase **for my sake**: it names a person, not a cause, as the safe place to lose yourself. Then, having asked for everything, the chapter closes at the smallest possible scale. Not martyrdom, not prophecy: **a cup of cold water** — ψυχροῦ, cold, the kind you had to fetch fresh — given to one unimportant person because they belong to him. That is the floor of the kingdom's economy, and the floor has a guarantee: *amen, he will certainly not lose his reward*. A chapter that begins by demanding your life ends by insisting that no kindness, however far below heroism, goes unrecorded. The scale runs from the cross down to a drink of water, and the whole of it counts.",
